WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.166
Think of a dictionary.

00:00:05.166 --> 00:00:08.333
Half a million words, and you never read them all.

00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.499
The tabs take you to the letter, the guide words to the page, and there is your word.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:14.665
AB-plus tree gives a database table that same stack of signposts.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:17.832
Hash indexes give constant time on equality and no help on ranges.

00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:20.998
AB-plus tree handles both with one shape.

00:00:20.998 --> 00:00:24.165
A hundred million songs, and you want Cruel Summer.

00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:27.331
Binary search would halve the list twenty-seven times.

00:00:27.331 --> 00:00:30.497
The database does it in four reeds.

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:33.664
The trick is one word.

00:00:33.664 --> 00:00:36.830
Fanout.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.980
Here is the whole structure.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.129
On the left are the data pages, the rows themselves on disk.

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:49.279
Finding a row means finding its page.

00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:53.428
The tree is built on top to get you there fast.

00:00:53.428 --> 00:00:57.578
Start at the root.

00:00:57.578 --> 00:01:01.727
It holds a few hundred keys in sorted order, and each key points to a subtree.

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:05.877
You compare your search key and follow the one pointer that covers it.

00:01:05.877 --> 00:01:10.026
That drops you into an internal node, the next level down, which narrows the range again.

00:01:10.026 --> 00:01:14.176
A few levels down you reach the leaves.

00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:18.325
The leaves hold every key in sorted order, and each one points straight to a data page.

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:22.475
So the path is root, internal, leaf, then the data page.

00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:26.624
Trace release date twelve thousand eight ninety.

00:01:26.624 --> 00:01:30.774
The root sends it into the middle range, the internal node into eleven thousand to thirteen thousand, the leaf resolves to page three, and page three holds the song, As It Was.

00:01:30.774 --> 00:01:34.923
Four reeds.

00:01:34.923 --> 00:01:39.073
Three for the tree, one for the data.

00:01:39.073 --> 00:01:43.222
A full scan would be millions.

00:01:43.222 --> 00:01:47.372
For a billion-tuple table at fanout one thousand the tree is just three levels deep, so doubling the data barely changes the depth.

00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:52.102
Now the part where B-plus trees pull ahead.

00:01:52.102 --> 00:01:56.831
Find every song whose release date falls between twelve thousand and fourteen thousand.

00:01:56.831 --> 00:02:01.561
Start at the root.

00:02:01.561 --> 00:02:06.290
Twelve thousand lands in the middle range, five thousand to fifteen thousand.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:11.020
Follow it to the internal node it points to.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.749
Its separators send you to the first leaf with a key at or above twelve thousand.

00:02:15.749 --> 00:02:20.479
From here you stop descending and scan the linked leaves in order.

00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:25.208
Leaf three holds twelve eight ninety and thirteen four fifty-six, both in range.

00:02:25.208 --> 00:02:29.938
Walk the link to leaf four, thirteen seven eighty-nine and thirteen nine eighty, both in range.

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:34.667
Now, why peek at leaf five at all?

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:39.397
Leaf four ended at thirteen nine eighty, still in range, so we cannot stop yet, the next leaf might have more.

00:02:39.397 --> 00:02:44.126
Leaf five opens with fourteen four fifty-six, past fourteen thousand, so we stop.

00:02:44.126 --> 00:02:48.856
And notice, that key is no accident.

00:02:48.856 --> 00:02:53.585
Fourteen four fifty-six is the exact separator the internal node held one level up to point at leaf five.

00:02:53.585 --> 00:02:58.315
The boundary was bracketed by the index the whole time.

00:02:58.315 --> 00:03:03.044
Then fetch the data pages the matching leaves point to, page three and page four.

00:03:03.044 --> 00:03:07.774
Five index reeds and two data reeds.

00:03:07.774 --> 00:03:12.503
Seven I Os total, against a full scan of the whole table.

00:03:12.503 --> 00:03:17.233
Sorted leaves linked end to end are why range queries are cheap on a B-plus tree and impossible on a hash index.

00:03:17.233 --> 00:03:21.076
Why so shallow?

00:03:21.076 --> 00:03:24.919
Binary search splits by two, and twenty-seven halvings find one song in a hundred million.

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:28.762
Now split by a hundred.

00:03:28.762 --> 00:03:32.605
After one reed ninety-nine percent of the data is gone.

00:03:32.605 --> 00:03:36.447
After two, ninety-nine point nine nine.

00:03:36.447 --> 00:03:40.290
That multiplier per level is fanout, and it is set by how many key and pointer pairs fit on one page.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.133
Real nodes hold hundreds of keys, so a billion tuples fit in three levels.

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:47.976
One more reed for the data page makes four.

00:03:47.976 --> 00:03:50.820
The rule is simple.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.665
AB-plus tree helps any time the database can use sorted order to narrow the search.

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:56.509
Equality narrows.

00:03:56.509 --> 00:03:59.354
Range narrows.

00:03:59.354 --> 00:04:02.198
Prefix narrows.

00:04:02.198 --> 00:04:05.042
Sorted output comes free.

00:04:05.042 --> 00:04:07.887
It does not help when the search cannot narrow.

00:04:07.887 --> 00:04:10.731
A function wrapping the column, since the index is on name, not on lower of name.

00:04:10.731 --> 00:04:13.576
A suffix match, with no prefix to start from.

00:04:13.576 --> 00:04:16.420
A not-equals, since almost every value qualifies.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.265
Each one falls back to a scan.

00:04:19.265 --> 00:04:22.109
Match the index to the query shape.

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:26.095
Almost every relational database uses B-plus trees as the default index.

00:04:26.095 --> 00:04:30.081
Point lookups, range scans, sorted output, prefix matches, one shape.

00:04:30.081 --> 00:04:34.067
But every write rebalances the tree, splitting nodes and writing both halves to disk.

00:04:34.067 --> 00:04:38.054
At a million writes a second that breaks.

00:04:38.054 --> 00:04:42.040
The next format is built for exactly that case.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:46.026
SS Tables.
